
================================================================================
README — Latent-Variable Batch-wise Optimization Control with Tube Feedback
          Example 2: Batch Crystallization  (GNU Octave)
================================================================================

This file accompanies the paper
  "Latent-Variable Batch-wise Optimization Control with Tube Feedback
   for Point-to-Point Tracking"
and contains the full GNU Octave simulation code for Example 2 (batch
crystallization): identification, uncertainty estimation, tube synthesis,
nominal batch-wise optimization, three online closed-loop controllers
(Proposed / Xue [30] / Oh [13]) and all reporting utilities.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. ENVIRONMENT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  * GNU Octave (tested on 11.x). MATLAB is NOT supported.
  * Required Octave packages:
        control   (dlqr, dlyap, observer utilities)
        optim     (qp)
        glpk      (usually shipped with Octave)
  * Load once per session:

        pkg load control
        pkg load optim

  * Working directory must be the folder that contains all *.m files.
    Every stage script resolves its own path via mfilename('fullpath')
    and adds it with addpath(project_dir); rehash().

  * Artefacts are stored in:

        results/stage01_setup.mat
        results/stage02_identification.mat
        results/stage03_tube.mat
        results/stage04_nominal.mat
        results/stage05_simulation.mat
        results/stage05_tube_checkpoint.mat
        results/reporting/                    (CSV tables, figures/)
        results/diagnostics/out/              (reviewer diagnostics)
        unreachable_results/                  (unreachable-reference case)


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. *** IMPORTANT WARNING — BEFORE YOU RUN ANYTHING ***
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  !!  DO NOT re-run run_stage01_setup.m ... run_stage05_simulation.m unless
  !!  you really need to. The full pipeline (Stage 2 with 300 training
  !!  batches, Stage 5 with the Oh [13] QP solver) takes roughly 1–2 hours.
  !!
  !!  If results/*.mat have been provided with the repository, START FROM
  !!  SECTION 4 (LOAD-BASED REPRODUCTION). Do NOT run Stages 1–5.

  Stage 5 alone is dominated by the Oh method: ~250 s per batch, 20 batches.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. FULL REPRODUCTION FROM SCRATCH (SLOW, ~1–2 h)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Run in this exact order from a fresh Octave prompt:

  STEP 1  ->  run_stage01_setup
              Builds Params, Reference, physical baseline.
              Saves results/stage01_setup.mat.

  STEP 2  ->  run_stage02_identification
              Generates 300 training + 5 validation + 5 calibration batches,
              fits preprocessing + static PLS + MIMO-ARX, runs CV,
              validates model.
              Saves results/stage02_identification.mat.

  STEP 3  ->  run_stage03_tube
              Builds innovation / reconstruction uncertainty sets,
              initial-state error set, LQR tube gain, RPI ellipsoid,
              physical constraint tightening, LP feasibility check.
              Saves results/stage03_tube.mat.

  STEP 4  ->  run_stage04_nominal
              Builds the common nominal problem, feasible initial point,
              optimal reachable point, finite nominal iterations.
              Saves results/stage04_nominal.mat.

  STEP 5  ->  run_stage05_simulation
              Runs Proposed (Tube), Xue [30] and Oh [13] closed-loop
              simulations on the shared scenario.
              Saves results/stage05_simulation.mat.

  STEP 6  ->  run_stage06_reporting
              Produces comparison CSV tables and Figures 10–17.
              Saves results/reporting/.

Command block:

    pkg load control; pkg load optim;
    addpath(pwd); rehash();

    run_stage01_setup
    run_stage02_identification
    run_stage03_tube
    run_stage04_nominal
    run_stage05_simulation
    run_stage06_reporting


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
4. LOAD-BASED REPRODUCTION (RECOMMENDED, FAST)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

If results/*.mat already exist, SKIP Stages 1–5. From a fresh Octave prompt
with the working directory at the repo root, run:

    pkg load control; pkg load optim;
    addpath(pwd); rehash();

    ----------------------------------------------------------------
    (4.1) All paper figures 10–17 and the comparison tables 16
    ----------------------------------------------------------------
        run_stage06_reporting
        -> reads results/stage05_simulation.mat
        -> writes results/reporting/comparison_table.csv
                 results/reporting/batch_metrics.csv
                 results/reporting/figures/fig1_tracking_comparison.*
                 results/reporting/figures/fig2_proposed_outputs.*
                 results/reporting/figures/fig3_proposed_inputs.*
                 results/reporting/figures/fig4_xue_outputs.*
                 results/reporting/figures/fig5_xue_inputs.*
                 results/reporting/figures/fig6_oh_outputs.*
                 results/reporting/figures/fig7_oh_inputs.*
                 results/reporting/figures/fig8_free_run_validation.*

    ----------------------------------------------------------------
    (4.2) Reviewer diagnostics: Tables 5, 9, 10, 11, 12 and Figure 18
    ----------------------------------------------------------------
        run_diagnostics_example2
        -> reads results/stage02_identification.mat
                 results/stage03_tube.mat
                 results/stage05_simulation.mat
        -> writes results/diagnostics/out/full_diagnostics_report.txt
                 results/diagnostics/out/figures/fig16_comparison.*
                 results/diagnostics/out/tables/table_params.txt
                 results/diagnostics/out/tables/table_reachable_points.txt
                 results/diagnostics/out/tables/active_constraints.txt

    ----------------------------------------------------------------
    (4.3) Unreachable-reference case: Figures 19, 20 and Table 17
    ----------------------------------------------------------------
        run_unreachable_example2
        -> overrides Params.y_ref_phys(2) = 0.013 and rebuilds the nominal
           problem, runs only the Proposed controller.
        -> writes unreachable_results/fig_unreachable_tracking.*
                 unreachable_results/fig_unreachable_trajectory_zoom.*
                 unreachable_results/table_unreachable_active.csv
                 unreachable_results/unreachable_example2_results.mat
        -> does NOT overwrite stage03_tube.mat or stage05_simulation.mat.

    ----------------------------------------------------------------
    (4.4) Oh [13] Q_trk / R_upd sensitivity: Table 14
    ----------------------------------------------------------------
        run_oh_sensitivity_sweep
        -> re-runs Oh [13] on 5 batches over a 3x3 (Q,R) grid.
           Runtime is roughly 9 x 5 x (Oh per-batch time) = 15–30 min.
        -> writes oh_sensitivity_results.txt
                 oh_sensitivity_workspace.mat

        sweep_main
        -> reads oh_sensitivity_workspace.mat and prints the
           max-adjacent-batch metrics.

    ----------------------------------------------------------------
    (4.5) Optional — Table 13 (A_lv x n_arx sensitivity)
    ----------------------------------------------------------------
        run_sensitivity_A
        -> *** SLOW: re-generates 300 training batches and retrains
              the model on a 5x3 grid. ***
        -> writes sensitivity_A_data.csv

        run_nominal_openloop_check
        -> console-only diagnostic on the nominal trajectories.

  RECOMMENDED MINIMUM to reproduce the paper's figures:
        run_stage06_reporting       (Figures 10–17, Table 16)
        run_diagnostics_example2    (Figure 18 with Theorem 3 bound)
        run_unreachable_example2    (Figures 19–20, Table 17)


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5. QUICK PIPELINE (ONLY when init_params.m has been edited)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

When you have modified only the controller parameters in init_params.m,
use the fast re-entry pipeline (it re-uses the existing Stage-2 model):

    run_fast_pipeline

This runs Stage 1 (to refresh Params/Reference), re-injects the new Params
into the saved Stage-2 model, then runs Stages 3, 4, 5, 6.

  NOTE: run_fast_pipeline will ERROR OUT if Params.A_lv or Params.n_arx
        has been changed — in that case Stage 2 must be re-run from scratch.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
